4. Addressing Model: How a Cue Selects a Memory

The benchmark is: write  m_1, \ldots, m_N , wait  \Delta  in the idle regime, present cue _i , read  \hat{m}_i . But how does the cue select memory  i  from a decayed, superposed chemical state? This is not a detail — it is the difference between two completely different theses.

4.1 Timed Readout (Reservoir Style)

The reservoir state at time  t_0+\Delta  is a fading superposition of all  N  writes. A query-conditioned decoder  D_\psi(y, q)  can only reconstruct what survives. With  N > 1  and large  \Delta , this collapses to reconstructing a mixture — serial recall at best.

4.2 Attractor Addressing (Hopfield Style)

The cue is injected as a perturbation and the dynamics converge to the nearest stored pattern. This is the only known mechanism for true content-addressed recall from a physical substrate.

Experimental evidence. DNA strand displacement cascades implement a Hopfield associative memory with four fully connected artificial neurons that, after training in silico, remembers four single-stranded DNA patterns and recalls the most similar one when presented with an incomplete pattern. More recently, DNA neural networks have been demonstrated to perform supervised learning in vitro, integrating molecular concentrations as memory during training and using that memory to process new test data.

4.3 Substrate-Specific Addressing Commitment

Substrate Addressing Model Physical Mechanism
DNA strand-displacement RNCRN Attractor-convergence recall Designed slow basins; Hopfield-style energy landscape
BZ array Timed/cued-decoder recall Spatiotemporal pattern superposition; amplitude-coded readout
Formose CSTR Timed readout (driven only) Flow-modulated concentration trajectories

Design implication. The RNCRN training procedure can literally design attractors with slow basins — this connects H1–H5 into one coherent program. The cue injection is part of the WRITE protocol (the quench), not a separate operation.

4.4 WRITE Channel and Dynamics Channel Are Not Independent

For both substrates, the actuator is the dynamics: in the BZ array, light both writes patterns and drives photosensitive chemistry; in the formose CSTR, the five input flows both encode and continuously reset the reservoir state (residence time = 2 min). The abstraction  u_t = E_\phi(m_t, q_t)  as a clean "write then walk away" protocol is physically false for the CSTR — stopping the flow is the transition into the idle regime, and whatever was written is flushed or frozen. The encoder must plan the trajectory of actuation, including the shutdown transient as part of the encoding. "How do I write so the system falls into the basin I want" is precisely attractor targeting.

