6. The Chemical Engram

A chemical memory is a distributed physical state together with its history. Define an engram:

E_i = (x_i, \tau_i, p_i, c_i)

where  x_i  = physical chemical state,  \tau_i  = temporal information,  p_i  = provenance,  c_i  = confidence.

\text{Memory} \neq \text{single stored value}

\text{Memory} = \text{state} + \text{history} + \text{dynamics}

6.1 Chemical State Fingerprinting

Because physical chemical systems drift, exact reproduction cannot be assumed. Define:

z_t = [c_1, \ldots, c_n, \dot{c}_1, \ldots, \text{phase}, \text{frequency}, \text{spatial pattern}, \ldots]

and a fingerprint:

F_t = \Phi(z_t)

The digital system stores  (\tau_i, p_i, c_i, F_t)  for each relevant chemical memory state.

6.2 Idle Decay Model

Critical correction (v0.5 → v0.6). The model  I(m; y_{\Delta}) = I_0 \cdot e^{-\Delta / \tau_{\mathrm{idle}}}  is a hypothesis, not a model — and likely the wrong functional family. Real chemical memory decays by distinct mechanisms:

1. Exponential relaxation to equilibrium.
2. Sudden collapse when the system falls into an attractor basin.
3. Periodic revival of information carried by phase (in which case  I(m; y_{\Delta})  vs.  \Delta  is non-monotonic).

The BZ-AOT hour-long storage works precisely because the pattern is pinned in a metastable state (bistable Turing patterns), not exponentially fading.

Proposal. Define  R(\Delta) = I(m; y_{\Delta})  nonparametrically in Phase Ic, then fit and compare candidate models (exponential, power-law, attractor-collapse step, oscillatory). The shape of  R(\Delta)  is itself a result — it tells you which theoretical framework governs the substrate.

6.3 Stochasticity

For DNA-implementable RNCRNs at nanomolar concentrations, copy-number noise makes retention probabilistic and the ODE picture a mean-field approximation. The chemical master equation is the exact description:

\frac{dP(\mathbf{n}, t)}{dt} = \sum_j \left[ a_j(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j) P(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j, t) - a_j(\mathbf{n}) P(\mathbf{n}, t) \right]

where  \mathbf{n}  is the copy-number vector,  \nu_j  are stoichiometry vectors, and  a_j  are propensity functions. At low copy numbers,  I(m; \hat{m})  becomes a random variable with variance scaling as  1/\sqrt{V} .

