8. Experimental Progression

Phase 0 — Capacity Screening and Regime-Split Estimation

Before any lab or simulator time, estimate  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{driven}}(\Delta)  and  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta)  for the two most mature candidates using data already in print.

BZ array: 5×5 addressable cells, amplitude-binned via image processing → rough entropy of the addressable space at a chosen  \Delta , discounted for reported drift. The raw addressable state count is  > 2.9 \times 10^{17} , corresponding to  \approx 58  bits of theoretical address space.

Formose reservoir: up to 106 ion channels at 500 ms resolution, with published forecast error growing over a  \delta t = 120  s horizon in the driven regime. The mutual information between input and ion signal as a function of lag is available in the published work.

Spectral characterization (new in v0.6). For each candidate, estimate:

· The Lyapunov spectrum  \{\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \ldots, \lambda_N\} , using the standard Benettin algorithm on the ODE model.
· The decay-mode structure: eigenvalues of the Jacobian at the attractor, ordered by real part.
· The ESP-compliance margin:  L = \sup_x \|Df(x)\| , the global Lipschitz constant.

Deliverable: a table of estimated  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{driven}}(\Delta)  and  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta)  upper bounds at several candidate  \Delta  (minutes / hours / days) per substrate, with explicit uncertainty ranges, plus the Lyapunov spectrum and decay-mode structure.

Phase I — Numerical Validation

Implement a differentiable RNCRN-based simulator. The RNCRN is a molecular version of a recurrent artificial neural network that can approximate arbitrary dynamics under suitable conditions. It is experimentally implementable with DNA strand displacement. The chemical system is exposed through a minimal interface:

```
WRITE(m)
READ(q)
OBSERVE(z)
```

The internal reaction mechanism remains hidden from the agent.

Phase Ia — Existence test: Jointly train  E_\phi  and  D_\psi  with a differentiable simulator.

Phase Ib — Learnability test: Freeze or hide  F , train the agent with model-free RL / evolution strategies / Bayesian optimization.

Phase Ic — Idle decay test: After writing  m , stop all forcing and measure  I(m; y_{\Delta})  as a function of idle delay  \Delta . Fit and compare candidate decay models (exponential, power-law, attractor-collapse step, oscillatory).

The comparison is:

M_D \quad \text{versus} \quad M_C \quad \text{versus} \quad M_D + M_C

on delayed associative recall with interference.

Phase II — Black-Box Interface Hardening

Introduce drift, sensor noise  \eta_t , forgetting, degradation, and state convergence. Test whether an agent can learn refreshing, redundancy, robust encoding, and robust retrieval.

Phase III — In Chemico Validation

Only after successful numerical validation should the learned interface be transferred to a physical microfluidic chemical reservoir. A suitable platform would be based on existing chemical reservoir computing architectures — the formose CSTR reservoir or a BZ array. The iontronic route (nanofluidic memristors with history-dependent conductance) offers a lower-energy alternative.

Phase IV — Demonstration of Advantage

Only if earlier hypotheses succeed should the research ask whether chemical dynamics provide a distinctive memory property.

