10. Falsifiable Hypotheses

H1 — Chemical Information Retention. Chemical state can retain task-relevant information after a delay  \Delta  in the idle regime. Operationally:

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) > 0

under specified conditions.

H2 — Learned WRITE Protocol. An agent can learn an effective WRITE protocol without requiring a manually designed encoding. Operationally: learned encoding improves recall over random stimulation by  > 20\%  absolute.

H3 — Learned READ Protocol. An agent can learn a useful READ protocol without explicit access to the chemical mechanism. Operationally:  D_\psi  recovers  >  chance from  y_{t-k:t} .

H4 — Hybrid Memory Advantage. Hybrid memory improves performance relative to capacity-matched digital memory under controlled conditions:

P(M_D^{\mathrm{matched}} + M_C) > P(M_D^{\mathrm{matched}})

with  p < 0.05  on long-horizon associative memory with interference.

H5 — Distinctive Physical Memory Regime. Only if H1–H4 succeed: does chemical dynamics provide a distinctive memory regime — temporal memory, attractor-based recall, associative convergence, graceful degradation — that provides a meaningful advantage over an equivalent digital baseline?

---

11. Failure Conditions

Failure A: Chemical dynamics do not retain useful information at the target delay in the idle regime. Then H1 = false.

Failure B: The chemical state contains information, but useful WRITE policies cannot be learned. Then H2 = false.

Failure C: WRITE is possible, but READ cannot recover useful information. Then H3 = false.

Failure D: Chemical memory works, but hybrid memory does not outperform capacity-matched digital memory. Then H4 = false.

Failure E: Hybrid memory works, but offers no distinctive physical advantage. Then H5 = false.

None of these outcomes invalidates the value of the experiment.

