Appendix C — Detailed Mathematical Derivations

C.1 Derivation of the Memory Capacity Expression

For a linear ESN  x_t = A x_{t-1} + C u_t  with  \rho(A) < 1 , the stationary state is:

x_t = \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C u_{t-j}

The covariance between  x_t  and  u_{t-\tau}  is:

\mathrm{Cov}(x_t, u_{t-\tau}) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C u_{t-j} u_{t-\tau} \right] = A^\tau C \sigma_u^2

The variance of the state is:

\Gamma_x = \mathrm{Var}(x_t) = \sigma_u^2 \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C C^\top (A^j)^\top

The optimal linear reconstruction of  u_{t-\tau}  from  x_t  is:

\hat{u}_{t-\tau} = \frac{\mathrm{Cov}(u_{t-\tau}, x_t)^\top \Gamma_x^{-1} x_t}{\sigma_u^2} u_{t-\tau}

The  \tau -lag memory capacity is the squared correlation:

\mathrm{MC}_\tau = \frac{\mathrm{Cov}(u_{t-\tau}, x_t)^\top \Gamma_x^{-1} \mathrm{Cov}(u_{t-\tau}, x_t)}{\sigma_u^2} = \sigma_u^2 \cdot C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \Gamma_x^{-1} A^\tau C

Summing over all lags:

\mathrm{MC} = \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} \mathrm{MC}_\tau = \sigma_u^2 \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \Gamma_x^{-1} A^\tau C = \sigma_u^2 \cdot \mathrm{tr}\left( \Gamma_x^{-1} \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} A^\tau C C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \right)

Since  \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} A^\tau C C^\top (A^\top)^\tau = \Gamma_x / \sigma_u^2 :

\mathrm{MC} = \sigma_u^2 \cdot \mathrm{tr}\left( \Gamma_x^{-1} \cdot \frac{\Gamma_x}{\sigma_u^2} \right) = \mathrm{tr}(I_N) = N

if  \Gamma_x  is invertible. If  \Gamma_x  has rank  r < N , then  \mathrm{MC} = r .

---

C.2 Derivation of the Decay Model from Spectral Decomposition

From Proposition B.6.1, the memory kernel is:

K(\tau) = \sum_{i,j} \alpha_{ij} (\lambda_i \lambda_j)^\tau

For a reservoir with a single dominant slow mode  \lambda_1 \approx 1  and all other modes fast ( |\lambda_i| \ll 1 ), the kernel is dominated by:

K(\tau) \approx \alpha_{11} |\lambda_1|^{2\tau}

Taking the logarithm:

\log K(\tau) \approx \log \alpha_{11} - 2\tau |\log |\lambda_1||

This is a linear decay in  \tau , corresponding to an exponential decay of mutual information:

I(m; y_\Delta) \approx I_0 e^{-\Delta / \tau_{\mathrm{idle}}}

where  \tau_{\mathrm{idle}} = 1 / (2 |\log |\lambda_1||) .

Remark C.2.1. For systems with multiple slow modes, the decay is a sum of exponentials, and the overall decay may exhibit power-law behavior if the eigenvalue density near  |\lambda| = 1  is continuous.

---

C.3 Fano Bound for the Chemical Reservoir

Combining Proposition 3.2 (Interface Capacity Bound) with Proposition B.3.1 (Fano Memory Bound):

\log M \leq \frac{C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) + h(\epsilon)}{1 - \epsilon}

For a target error probability  \epsilon = 0.05  and  h(0.05) \approx 0.199 :

\log M \leq \frac{C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) + 0.199}{0.95}

If  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) = 1  nat (a modest channel capacity), then:

\log M \leq \frac{1 + 0.199}{0.95} \approx 1.26 \text{ nats} \approx 1.82 \text{ bits}

so  M \leq e^{1.26} \approx 3.5  distinguishable memory states.

If  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) = 5  nats (a high capacity for a physical substrate), then:

\log M \leq \frac{5 + 0.199}{0.95} \approx 5.47 \text{ nats} \approx 7.9 \text{ bits}

so  M \leq e^{5.47} \approx 237  memory states.

These bounds are sobering: even a relatively high-capacity chemical channel supports only a few hundred reliably distinguishable memory states at 5% error. This underscores the importance of Phase 0 capacity estimation.

