Appendix F — Information-Theoretic Bounds for Specific Substrates

F.1 BZ Array Capacity Estimate

The BZ array has 25 cells, each with 2 states (on/off), giving  2^{25} = 3.3 \times 10^7  addressable states. The proposal cites  2.9 \times 10^{17}  chemical states, which accounts for additional degrees of freedom per cell (amplitude, phase, etc.).

Addressable bits:  \log_2(2.9 \times 10^{17}) \approx 58  bits.

Usable bits after discounting: If observation bandwidth allows discrimination of  K  levels per cell, the usable state count is  K^{25} . For  K = 4 :

N_{\mathrm{usable}} = 4^{25} = 2^{50} \approx 1.1 \times 10^{15}

so  \log_2 N_{\mathrm{usable}} = 50  bits.

After discounting for noise, drift, and partial observability (assume 50% loss):

C_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx 25 \text{ bits}

F.2 Formose CSTR Capacity Estimate

The formose reservoir has 106 ion channels at 500 ms resolution. The published forecast horizon is 120 s. The mutual information between input and ion signal as a function of lag is available in the published work.

Driven capacity: The published data suggest  I(u; y_\Delta)  decays over 120 s. Extrapolating to  \Delta = 1  hour is not justified without idle-regime data.

Idle capacity: No published data. Phase 0 must estimate upper bounds from physical constraints (residence time, diffusion, reaction kinetics).

